.TH C5XHEX 1 "2026-10-05" "c5xtools 1.0.1" "c5xtools Manual"
.SH NAME
c5xhex \- COFF-to-hex / ROM image converter for the TMS320C5x DSP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B c5xhex
.I in.out
.B \-f
.IR format
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-romwidth
.IR 8 | 16 ]
.RB [ \-order
.IR lsb | msb ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-image
.RB [ \-org
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-len
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-fill
.IR val ]]
.RB [ \-V | \-q | \-h | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B c5xhex
reads an executable TMS320C5x TI COFF
.I .out
file (as produced by
.BR c5xlnk (1))
and writes its loadable contents as a programmer/ROM image in one of several
text or binary formats. It is the final step that turns a linked program into
something a device programmer, boot loader or simulator can consume.
.PP
Two broad modes are available. In the default mode the loadable section words
are emitted as they are. In image mode
.RB ( \-image )
a single contiguous block covering an explicit address range is emitted, with
any addresses not occupied by a section filled with a constant, which is what
a fixed-size ROM part expects.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I in.out
The executable COFF file to convert. Exactly one input is required.
.TP
.BI \-f " format"
Select the output format. Required. One of:
.RS
.TP
.B intel
Intel-HEX records (text).
.TP
.B srec
Motorola S-record text.
.TP
.B tagged
TI-tagged object text.
.TP
.B ascii
Plain ASCII hex words, for quick inspection.
.TP
.B bin
Raw binary bytes, no records or addresses.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the output to
.IR file .
The default is standard output. For the
.B bin
format a file is recommended, since the output is raw binary.
.TP
.BI \-romwidth " 8|16"
Set how wide a stored word is in the output stream.
.B 16
(the default) emits the full 16-bit word as two bytes.
.B 8
emits one byte per word (the low byte), the classic layout for an 8-bit-wide
ROM. This changes how many bytes each program word occupies in the image.
.TP
.BI \-order " lsb|msb"
Choose the byte order used when a 16-bit word is split into bytes.
.B lsb
emits the least-significant byte first,
.B msb
the most-significant first. The default is
.B lsb
in the normal mode; in image mode the default is
.BR msb .
It affects only the ordering of bytes within each word, not which words are
present.
.TP
.BI \-e " addr"
Record
.I addr
as the entry-point address in formats that carry one. Ignored by formats that
have no entry record.
.TP
.B \-image
Emit one contiguous ROM image rather than the section words alone. The range
is given by
.B \-org
and
.BR \-len ,
and any word in that range not provided by a section is set to the
.B \-fill
value. Use this to produce a complete, fixed-size ROM.
.TP
.BI \-org " addr"
The starting word address of the image. Required with
.BR \-image .
.TP
.BI \-len " n"
The length of the image in words. Required with
.BR \-image .
Together with
.B \-org
it fixes the address window that is emitted.
.TP
.BI \-fill " val"
The 16-bit value written for every word in the image range that no section
covers. The default is
.BR 0 .
Only meaningful with
.BR \-image .
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-verbose
Report the sections read and the byte counts written, to standard error.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Reduce the start-up identity banner to a single line.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit successfully.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and identity banner and exit successfully.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert a linked program to Intel-HEX on standard output:
.PP
.EX
c5xhex app.out \-f intel
.EE
.PP
Write Motorola S-records to a file:
.PP
.EX
c5xhex app.out \-f srec \-o app.s19
.EE
.PP
Produce an 8-bit-wide, least-significant-byte-first hex image (classic ROM
byte layout):
.PP
.EX
c5xhex app.out \-f intel \-romwidth 8 \-order lsb \-o app.hex
.EE
.PP
Burn a complete 4K-word ROM starting at 0x8000, filling gaps with 0xFFFF:
.PP
.EX
c5xhex app.out \-f intel \-image \\
       \-org 0x8000 \-len 0x1000 \-fill 0xFFFF \-o rom.hex
.EE
.PP
Emit a raw binary image:
.PP
.EX
c5xhex app.out \-f bin \-o app.bin
.EE
.SS Full toolchain flow
The three tools chain together: assemble each source to a COFF object with
.BR c5xasm (1),
link the objects into an image with
.BR c5xlnk (1),
then turn that image into a ROM or programmer format such as Intel-HEX with
.BR c5xhex (1).
.PP
Absolute build (the usual case: fixed load addresses, directly loadable),
ending in a standard Intel-HEX file:
.PP
.EX
c5xasm main.asm \-coff \-o main.obj  # source -> COFF object
c5xasm dsp.asm  \-coff \-o dsp.obj
c5xlnk main.obj dsp.obj \-o app.out  # objects -> absolute .out
c5xhex app.out \-f intel \-o app.hex  # .out -> Intel-HEX image
.EE
.PP
Relocatable build (partially link now, finish the absolute link later):
.PP
.EX
c5xasm a.asm \-coff \-o a.obj
c5xasm b.asm \-coff \-o b.obj
c5xlnk a.obj b.obj \-r \-o part.obj   # relocatable, re-linkable
c5xlnk part.obj \-o app.out          # final absolute link
c5xhex app.out \-f srec \-o app.s19   # S-record alternative
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A read error on the input, or an output error.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: missing input, a missing or unknown output format
.RB ( \-f ),
.B \-image
without
.B \-org
and
.BR \-len ,
or an unknown option.
.SH NOTES
A linked
.I .out
is a container: headers, a symbol table and the section data. A device
programmer or a boot ROM wants none of that \- only the program words at their
addresses.
.B c5xhex
discards the container and emits just those words in a form the target
accepts.
.PP
.B \-romwidth
and
.B \-order
exist because memory parts differ. If the target memory is eight bits wide,
each 16-bit program word is stored as a single byte stream
.RB ( \-romwidth\ 8 );
if it is sixteen bits wide, both bytes are emitted
.RB ( \-romwidth\ 16 ,
the default).
.B \-order
selects which byte of a word comes first in the stream, and it must match how
the hardware or the programmer reassembles the word \- choose wrongly and the
device sees byte-swapped or half-missing code.
.PP
By default only the addresses a section actually occupies are emitted. A
fixed-size ROM, however, must be fully specified, so
.B \-image
emits every word across the
.BR \-org .. \-org + \-len
window and sets the untouched words to
.BR \-fill ;
the result then matches the exact size and contents the part will hold.
.PP
For interchange with a programmer,
.B intel
(Intel-HEX) and
.B srec
(Motorola S-record) are the usual choices;
.B tagged
and
.B ascii
are convenient for inspection, and
.B bin
is the raw image with no addressing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR c5xasm (1),
.BR c5xlnk (1),
.BR c5xdis (1)
